## Optional services
# Which optional services Compose manages: `smoking-pi enable NAME` and
# `disable NAME` change it (`smoking-pi enable` alone lists them). It must
# list EVERY profile you rely on, because it is what `up` and `down` read.
# Starting a service once with `COMPOSE_PROFILES=mcp docker compose up -d
# mcp-server` leaves it running but UNMANAGED: the next `docker compose down`
# removes it and the following `up -d` does not bring it back. Nothing warns
# you — the MCP server just stops answering and OpenClaw silently falls back
# to shell commands.
#   influxdb | clickhouse  (pick one, matching TSDB_TYPE)
#   alerts                 (alerting engine — docs/alerting.md)
#   mcp                    (MCP server — docs/mcp-server.md)
#   ai                     (AI health reports — docs/ai-insights.md)
#   dns                    (DNS observer on port 53 — docs/dns-observer.md;
#                           `smoking-pi dns enable` sets it up)
#   inference              (EXPERIMENTAL congestion and degradation detectors
#                           — docs/inference.md)
COMPOSE_PROFILES=influxdb

## State locations
# Where the stack keeps the state it rewrites (docs/packaging.md, "Relocatable
# state"). Unset, everything stays beside this file, untracked by git:
#   SMOKING_PI_CONFIG_DIR   targets/probes/sources YAML the stack edits
#                           (default ./config-manager/config; an empty
#                           directory is seeded on first start)
#   SMOKING_PI_OUTPUT_DIR   generated SmokePing Targets/Probes
#                           (default ./config-manager/output; Pro only)
# A packaged install sets them to /etc/smoking-pi/config and
# /var/lib/smoking-pi/output. Paths are relative to this directory. The
# env file itself is relocated with SMOKING_PI_ENV_FILE, read by setup.sh,
# show-passwords.sh and the smoking-pi command (docker compose --env-file).
#SMOKING_PI_CONFIG_DIR=
#SMOKING_PI_OUTPUT_DIR=

## MCP server
# MCP server (opt-in: `smoking-pi enable mcp` — see docs/mcp-server.md).
# Bearer token required on the HTTP transport; empty = unauthenticated.
# install generates it; `smoking-pi passwords --show-secrets` prints it. To rotate:
# openssl rand -hex 32
#: secret install
MCP_API_TOKEN=
# Address the MCP server listens on. Empty = 127.0.0.1, which is right when
# OpenClaw runs on this machine or reaches it through an SSH tunnel. Set it to
# this host's tailnet IP only for the direct-bind variant in
# docs/remote-openclaw.md; NEVER 0.0.0.0 -- the bearer token was designed to
# guard a loopback port from other local processes, not a public one.
MCP_HOST=
# Remote assistants: the HTTPS address a tunnel publishes this MCP server at,
# e.g. https://mcp.example.com (no path). Empty = off. When set, any assistant
# that can add a remote MCP server (Claude, ChatGPT, Grok...) connects with
# `sudo smoking-pi connect NAME`: a URL and a one-time pairing code. It gets
# the read-only tools only; `smoking-pi disconnect NAME` cuts it off.
# MCP_HOST stays 127.0.0.1: the tunnel runs on this host.
# See docs/remote-connector.md
#: origin
MCP_PUBLIC_URL=

## Links
# Deep links in MCP tool responses (and the web-admin AI chat): the address a
# person reading the answer can actually open. Leave empty and the tools return
# numbers without links — which is deliberate, since this host answers on a LAN
# IP, a Tailscale name and possibly a tunnel hostname, and a link to the wrong
# one is worse than no link at all.
#
# Set the host and the standard ports are appended (:3000 Grafana, :8080
# web-admin):
#   PUBLIC_BASE_HOST=192.168.1.10
#   PUBLIC_BASE_HOST=smoking-pi.local   (the mdns service's name: `smoking-pi
#                                        links --lan mdns` stores the one it holds)
#   PUBLIC_BASE_HOST=smokingpi.tailnet-name.ts.net
# An IPv6 literal is bracketed for you (2001:db8::5 -> http://[2001:db8::5]:3000);
# a link-local fe80:: one is ignored, since no browser opens it.
# Or set the full URLs, for a reverse proxy or Cloudflare tunnel where the
# ports are not part of the address (these win over PUBLIC_BASE_HOST):
#   GRAFANA_PUBLIC_URL=https://grafana.example.com
#   WEB_ADMIN_PUBLIC_URL=https://admin.example.com
PUBLIC_BASE_HOST=
GRAFANA_PUBLIC_URL=
WEB_ADMIN_PUBLIC_URL=

# The same three again, for the address that works from OUTSIDE the home
# network — a Cloudflare tunnel, typically. Set both tiers and every link is
# offered twice: the LAN one (faster, and up even when the tunnel isn't) and
# the from-anywhere one, so an answer read on cellular still has a link that
# opens. Set only these and they become the only links.
#   smoking-pi tunnel start    # prints them; `smoking-pi tunnel` again later
#
# Quick tunnels print a fresh *.trycloudflare.com hostname on every restart —
# fine to paste here for a while, but a named tunnel is what you want if the
# links are going into alerts.
# Include the scheme. A bare host gets http:// and the service port appended
# (:3000, :8080), which is right for a LAN address and wrong for every tunnel
# — they answer https on 443. `TUNNEL_BASE_HOST=smokingpi.example.com` becomes
# http://smokingpi.example.com:3000 and produces a dead link that looks fine.
#   TUNNEL_BASE_HOST=https://smokingpi.example.com
#   GRAFANA_TUNNEL_URL=https://grafana.example.com
#   WEB_ADMIN_TUNNEL_URL=https://admin.example.com
TUNNEL_BASE_HOST=
GRAFANA_TUNNEL_URL=
WEB_ADMIN_TUNNEL_URL=

